s an emerging and promising material candidate for gas sensing purposes, metal organic frameworks (MOFs)1,2 have drawn widespread attention due to their high surface areas, tunable properties, and abundant active sites. However, challenges still exist for the general and extensive application of MOFs materials in chemical sensors. For example, the skeletal structure of some MOFs may collapse in heavy moisture or extremely reactive chemical conditions due to a lack of chemical stability, and more importantly, most MOFs have poor electrical conductivity,3,4 which disabled the acquisition of chemiresistive sensing signals. As a result, capacitance,5,6 impedance,7,8 and fluorescence9−12 sensors were more frequently demonstrated from MOFs materials. The emergence of 2D conductive $\mathbf{MOF}^{13-17}$ brings their potentials in the fabrication of chemiresistors. In 2015, Dinca18 and coworkers first used 2D conductive MOFs $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\big(\mathrm{HITP}\big)_{2}$ in chemiresistive sensors to detect ammonia gas below the ppm level. Since then, more 2D conductive MOF materials have
been employed in chemical sensing.13,16,19,20 However, the 2D conductive MOF material has fewer types and is complicated in synthesis. The ligands of 2D conductive MOF have mainly been concentrated in conjugated benzene rings, such as triphenylene,19−22 polysubstituted benzen e,23−25 and porphyrin rings.26,27 Highly active ligands usually lead to the overly fast assembly and therefore the production of amorphous products in hydrothermal synthesis. Recently, attempts have been made to find applications for easily acquired amorphous MOF28−30 materials. For example, Ge and co-workers encapsulated enzymes in amorphous MOFs by one-pot coprecipitation method, which showed 5−20 times higher apparent activity compared to crystalline MOFs encapsulation. Li et al.30 introduced missing linkers into a layered-pillared MOF CoBDC, which exhibited excellent OER performance with ultralow overpotential. The excellent performance of amorphous MOF in catalysis inspires us to apply it in chemical gas sensing.
Humidity detection has attracted tremendous attention due to its wide and essential application in industry,31−33 environmental protection,34,35 and personal life.36−38 For the last few decades, many materials have been developed to improve the performance of humidity sensors, such as traditional conductive polymers,39−43 ceramics, $^{44-47}$ metal oxide semiconductors,48−53 carbon based materials,54−63 and so on. However, few of these sensory materials can satisfy the full requirements of simple operation, specific recognition, high sensitivity/stability, and fast response and recovery at the same time. For example, polymer materials have the advantages of diversity and low working temperature, but the long-term stability is still rather unsatisfactory to meet the demand for practical applications. Ceramics44,45 and metal oxides48−53 are good candidates for humidity sensors because of their superior sensing properties and thermal stability, but their functions usually require external heating. Therefore, rational design and synthesis of new materials are urgently needed for high performance humidity sensors.
Here we demonstrate the synthesis of series of intrinsically amorphous, missing-linker 2D conductive MOF through ligand (hexaaminobenzene, HAB) oxidation, and their effective applications in chemical sensing. The use of defective ligand leads to the missing-linker structure and the amorphous morphology in Ni-HAB, but with retained conductivity. The natural defects in the resulting amorphous Ni-HAB (aNiHAB) can also serve as the interaction site for fast and reversible water adsorption. A novel aMOF-based chemiresistor was then developed for sensitive and fast humidity detection, in sharp contrast to corresponding crystalline MOF, achieving an excellent linear response in $20{-}90\%$ relative humidity (RH), and the response time and recovery time of 5.43 and 1.31 s, which is a record low compared to other organic-based chemiresistive humidity sensors (as shown in Table 1).
## SYNTHESIS OF CONDUCTIVE 2D MOF WITH MISSING-LINKER CHARACTERISTIC
Crystalline Ni-HAB (crystal structure illustrated in Figure 1a) was synthesized according to previous reports, and missinglinker Ni-HAB (Figure 1b) was synthesized by the hydrothermal method. Synthetic details can be found in Experimental Section and Figure S2; all DI $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was thoroughly degassed. Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images (Figure S3a) of the as-prepared sample shows that the 2D MOF samples are stacked in flakes. Additional spectroscopic characterizations were applied to further confirm the structure of the material. The FTIR spectrum is shown in Figure S3c; peaks at 645 and $1606~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ can be assigned to the aromatic ring, the strong signal at $\sim1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ matches to the $_{\mathrm{C-N}}$ stretching, and peaks at 860 and $1022~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ are attributed to the in-plane bending of unsubstituted $\mathrm{C-H}$ in benzene.64 The vibration peaks of $\mathrm{{C-H}}$ $\left(3000{-}3100\ \ \mathrm{~cm^{-}}\right)$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ $(3100{-}3500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ stretching were not observed probably due to the broad hydroxyl absorption peak. XPS (Figure S3b) shows the presence of N 1s, Ni 2p, and C 1s peaks and the absence of other counterions, confirming the formation of the neutral Ni-HAB. Importantly, the elemental ratio of nickel to nitrogen was found to be close to 1:2.9, in which the nitrogen content is evidently less than expected in cNi-HAB with perfect crystalline structure $\left(\mathrm{Ni}{:}\mathrm{N}=1{:}4\right)$ ). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) revealed that aNi-HAB decomposes above 200 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S4a). Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area is determined to be $159.906~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for aNi-HAB (Figure S4b). The average pore size is $2.23~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is three times as previously reported.
Hexaaminobenzene (HAB) is a typical multisubstituted ligand that leads to the construction of 2D MOF structure, as illustrated in Figure 1a. Due to the intrinsic electron conductivity, this material is widely used in batteries,64 capacitors,65 and catalysis.28,30 Typically, due to the instability of the ligand itself, most related MOF preparation requires careful protection, and as-prepared materials often have a large number of defect sites and poor crystalline level. Interestingly, we consider that for chemical sensing purposes, the increased presence of defect sites may be more beneficial to the molecular adsorption and desorption. Therefore, oxidized HAB was intentionally selected as the starting ligand to synthesize 2D Ni-HAB materials with multiple defect sites. NMR and LC-MS characterization of oxidized and pure HAB (Figure S1) indicate that the oxidized ligand is a mixture probably consisted of hexaaminobenzene, pentaaminobenzene, and tetraaminobenzne, where pentaaminobenzene is the major content. In addition, FT-IR of oxidized HAB showing $\mathrm{C-H}$ vibration further supports the depletion of amino groups. For the convenience of further theoretical calculation and mechanistic investigation, pentaaminobenzene was chosen as the ligand to propose the model of a defective aNi-HAB structure (that fits the final Ni to N ratio), as illustrated in Figure 1b. Due to the defective sites from the missing amine groups on the ligands, the aNi-HAB shows multiple unsaturated, missing-linker characteristics, which also leads to the amorphous morphology.
## RELATIVE HUMIDITY DETECTION WITHMISSING-LINKER ANI-HAB
Chemiresistive gas sensor devices were fabricated by dropcasting (Figure 1d). SEM image shows that aNi-HAB is closely packed in a thin film on the silicon wafer (Figure 1e). Interestingly, powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern (Figure 1f) shows that the material lacks long-range ordering, indicating an amorphous structure compared with cNi-HAB. The sensing signals were created by exposing the aNi-HAB device to pulses of analyte with varying concentrations, and recorded by a Keithley 2602B source-measure-unit (SMU). The target analyte (relative humidity) was generated by flowing the carrier gas (high purity dry $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{N}_{2}\dot{},}\end{array}$ ) into a bubbler containing DI water (Figure S2d). The crystalline cNi-HAB is prepared by the same method as for the sensing test, and the results are shown in Figure S5.
The chemiresistive response of aNi-HAB to different level of relative humidity (RH) were tested, and the data (Figure 2a) indicated that conductive current of the aNi-MOF devices demonstrate significant increase upon exposure to $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at the concentration range from $20\%$ to $90\%$ RH (diluted in $\mathrm{N}_{2,}^{\cdot\mathrm{~\cdot~}}$ ). The detection range of $20\%$ to $90\%$ RH at room temperature meets the sensitivity requirement for a variety of daily life applications. When the humidity pulse was replaced by dry ${\bf N}_{2}$ background, the current of the aNi-HAB device underwent a full recovery, to a broad range of RH from $20\%$ to $90\%$ . The signal level is $_{1-3}$ orders of magnitude larger than the noise, indicating a promising potential to further decrease the limit of detection (LOD). The response calibration curve is presented in Figure 2b, which exhibited good linearity of humidity response (linear sensitivity) to a broad RH range $(20-70\%$ RH). At RH above $70\%,$ , the response shows another linear region. The first linear region can be explained by the hydrogen bonding between the water molecules with defective and hydroxylated metal sites, and the second linear region can be rationalized by the strong coordination between water molecules and metal ions. In sharp contrast to the aNi-HAB, crystalline cNi-HAB only responds at high relative humidity (above $60\%$ ), with a considerably low linearity. The obviously enhanced RH sensitivity from aNi-HAB compared to cNi-HAB indicates certain benefits to water molecular recognition from the missing-linker characteristic in the conductive MOF structure. More importantly, these defect sites (and consequent hydrogen bonding to the analyte molecules) offer unique advantages in the response and recovery time of aNiHAB. As shown in Figure 2c, for a typical aNi-HAB device, the response time for $80\%$ RH was determined to be ${5.43\pm1.28\ s},$ whereas the corresponding recovery time is $1.31\ \pm\ 0.22\ s,$ which is significantly faster in RH detection than cNi-HAB. Such fast response/recovery times are unprecedented in organic-based materials, and are presumably the result of fast adsorption/desorption behavior of water molecules at the defect sites in the aNi-HAB structure, based on hydrogen bonding. The repeating response of the aNi-HAB sensor to rapid variations in the dry air and $80\%$ RH is shown in Figure 2d. We tested 20 cycles under $80\%$ RH, and an excellent reversibility was observed.
For a systematic evaluation of the missing-linker conductive MOF for RH detection, we further prepared a series of similar 2D MOF materials with different metal ions (Ni, Cu, Co, Fe)
and fabricated corresponding sensory devices via the same approach. The test results for different RH and VOC levels are shown in Figure 3. We observed that aCu-MOF (Figure 3b) has a higher response to water, ethanol, and acetone than other MOFs. The relative response can be enhanced by 2 to 3 orders of magnitude, but the selectivity to different gases is relatively poor. On the other hand, aCo-MOF and aFe-MOF (Figure 3c,d) show better selectivity among different gases, but the relative response is weaker and the signal-to-noise ratio is relatively larger. In general, aNi-MOF (Figure 3a) shows the best balance between sensitivity and selectivity, which should be related to the larger number of defective sites in the aNiMOF (Figures S6−7).
When compared with other MOF-based humidity sensors,2,5,8,66−73 aNi-HAB shows the fastest response and recovery behavior and excellent overall performance (summarized in Table 1). All of these results demonstrate a promising potential of aNi-HAB, or the defective amorphous conductive MOF materials in general, for the application of high-performance chemiresistive sensing. The missing-linker structure induced fast adsorption/desorption dynamics that can serve as a general strategy to solve the problem of slow response in MOF-based organic chemical sensors.
The abundant hydroxyl functional groups at the missinglinker sites in aNi-HAB (Figure 1b) certainly determines its unique high RH sensing performance. Therefore, further functionalization using Nafion, which is an excellent cation exchanger composed of perfluorosulfonate groups, can potentially offer a good adsorption capacity for water molecules toward enhanced sensitivity. Typically, a Nafion layer was deposited on top of the as-prepared MOF film, as illustrated in Figure 4a. We tested a series of Nafion mass fractions and measured their tailored response to humidity.
The result is shown in Figure $\boldsymbol{4\mathrm{b}}$ ; with optimal Nafion mass fraction of $1\%$ , aNi-HAB sensor’s response to humidity can be enhanced significantly, with better signal-to-noise ratio and lower detection limit. Moreover, a better linear correlation is achieved in the calibration curve from Nafion modified aNiHAB, which can be attributed to the better water adsorption capacity of Nafion membrane than pristine aNi-HAB, further leading to more stabilized adsorption (linear response). We continued to study the effect of the Nafion functionalization on the sensing selectivity; results are demonstrated in Figure 4c. Nafion modification improved the response to the three tested molecules in general. In addition, the selectivity of water to organic vapors is significantly increased.
## MECHANISM EVALUATIONS OF ULTRAFAST HUMIDITY SENSING WITH MISSING-LINKER ANI-HAB
There are generally two types of mechanisms applied to humidity sensing. One is through proton conduction, also named as Grotthuss-type mechanism,7,8,54 and the other includes interactions between water molecules and functional hydroxyl groups on the surface, grain boundary, and interface between sensing material and electrodes, which reduce the potential barrier and the activation energy of the sensing material, thereby causing changes in sensing signals such as conductance, resistance, impedance, and capacitance13,16,18 Compared with the ideal crystalline structure (Figure 1a), coordination of pentaaminobenzene with metal ions produces a large number of hydroxylated defect sites in a-Ni-HAB (Figure 1b). Fast nucleation speed was not suitable for large crystal growth, resulting in a less densely packed mesostructure, and the long-range ordering deteriorate. Because the material was synthesized in an alkaline environment, it is reasonable to speculate that hydroxyl groups replace the amino group for coordination to make a more stable structure (Figure 1b). As a result, there are a large number of hydroxyl and amino groups in the material that can be adsorbed and desorbed with water molecules through hydrogen bonding, which could bring an intrinsic ability for proton transport.
In order to explore the sensing mechanism of aNi-HAB and cNi-HAB, transient $I{-}t$ curves under different relative humidities were tested with $_{\mathrm{~1~V~}}$ DC voltage, and results are shown in Figure 5. When the DC voltage is applied to aNiHAB (presaturated with RH), the current decays exponentially, and finally reaches a plateau that is $_{1-3}$ orders (depending on RH) of magnitude larger than the baseline value (Figure 5a). This result demonstrated that the total conduction current within aNi-HAB includes both ionic conduction (which decays exponentially due to the interfacial charging at electrodes) and electron conduction (steady state current). In ionically conductive MOF materials,74,75 transport of ionic/protonic carriers can be highly dependent on the adsorption of water molecules, and therefore, our result implies a proton transport-based RH sensing mechanism. On the contrary, a similar proton conduction characteristic in cNiHAB was not observed (as shown in Figure 5b), and the RH sensing signals might originate from charge-transfer-based mechanism (upon water adsorption and electronic interaction). It should be noted that the latter pathway might also exist in aNi-HAB as well (less likely due to its metallic electronic structure), but its ultrafast response and recovery are probably a result of the proton-based mechanism. Higher sensitivity of the aNi-HAB can be attributed to more abundant active sites and lower charge carrier density compared to cNiHAB.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculations for electronic band structure further supported our conclusions. The DFT results suggested a semiconducting nature of the cNi-HAB monolayer, as shown in Figure S8. The projected density of states (PDOS) displayed main contributions from Ni, C, and N orbitals around the Fermi level, indicating the high degree of in-plane $\pi$ -conjugation. Interestingly, with the introduction of the hydroxyl groups, the O orbitals also made the contributions around the Fermi level, showing a switched metallic behavior in aNi-HAB. We further calculated the extent of charge transfer $(\delta_{\mathrm{e}})$ between adsorbed $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules and Ni-HAB layers, as listed in Figure 6. The electrons can transfer from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules to the cNi-HAB layers with $\delta_{\mathrm{e}}$ of $-0.02\ e$ at both Ni-site and N-site. The $\mathbf{n}$ -type doping of water molecules increases the electron density in the material, which is consistent with the observed signal in $\mathbf{n}$ -type cNi-HAB semiconductors in nitrogen atmosphere.76 In the aNi-HAB case, the direction of electron transfer is opposite, from the aNi-HAB layers to the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules at Ni-1-site $\left(\delta_{\mathrm{e}}=0.14\right)$ e) and Ni-2-site $\left(\delta_{\mathrm{e}}~=~0.10~e\right)$ with the hydroxyl defects. However, the electron transfer in aNi-HAB is less likely to contribute to the signaling in RH detection, due to the metallic nature of the aNi-HAB.
Computational simulations were further employed to provide a mechanistic understanding and rationalization of the excellent sensing performance of aNi-HAB, especially for the significant rapid response/recovery that cannot be simply explained by stronger binding between water molecules and the hydroxyl groups at the defect site. The geometries of cNiHAB and aNi-HAB were optimized by using density functional theory (DFT), as shown in Figure 6. It revealed that the local symmetry of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions is $D_{4h},$ leading to planar monolayers of cNi-HAB. However, the symmetry was broken by missinglinker and hydroxyl defect sites in aNi-HAB, giving rise to a nonplanar distorted monolayer. Possible binding sites were calculated to explore the binding strength between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules and Ni-HAB. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule can bind with cNiHAB by Ni-coordination and hydrogen bonding interaction, corresponding to Ni-site and N-site, respectively. The binding energy was $-0.03\ \mathrm{eV}$ at Ni-site with the coordination distance of 3.09 Å. At N-site, two $\mathrm{N-H\cdotsO}$ hydrogen bonds were 2.30 and 2.41 Å with the overall binding energy of $-0.12\ \mathrm{eV}$ . In aNi-HAB, with the number of hydroxyl groups at the Ni-site increasing from 0 to 2, the binding energies can be increased to $-0.80\ \mathrm{\eV},$ as shown in Figure 6. Meanwhile, the binding energy of N-site can also be strengthened (from $-0.12$ to $-0.56\ \mathrm{eV},$ ) by the extra hydrogen bonding at the defects. In conclusion, the introduction of multiple hydroxyls significantly increases the number of adsorption sites in aMOF and also the corresponding binding energies for water adsorption, leading to enhanced sensing signals especially at the low RH level (i.e., a wider response range). More importantly, the additional binding sites as a result of the hydroxyl-related hydrogen bonding will increase the available transition pathways during the surface adsorption process, effectively reducing the activation energy and making it easier for adsorption to reach equilibrium. This serves as the fundamental basis for the significantly improved response time in missing-linker 2D MOFs.
In conclusion, we have demonstrated a novel and facile synthesis of missing-linker amorphous MOF aNi-HAB with multiple defects. The presence of multiple hydroxylated defect sites allows the water molecules to undergo rapid adsorption and desorption through hydrogen bonding, exhibiting a record fast response and recovery in the detection of relative humidity compared to cNi-HAB and other MOF-based humidity sensors, and exhibits excellent linear response in $20{-}90\%$ relative humidity and superb stability, as well as convenient preparation. The selectivity and other sensor performance can be further improved over Nafion functionalization. Mechanistic investigation and DFT calculations further revealed the origin of the excellent sensing performance in the missing-linker aNiHAB. Our study here represents a new design principle for the development of conductive 2D MOF with fast response/ recovery, which has been a general issue for organic-based chemical sensory devices.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Fabrication of the Sensory Devices. The as-synthesized aNiHAB sample was dispersed in deionized water to form a $0.5~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ solution, then drop-casted onto a Si chip with prepatterned Ti−Au $\left({20}/{50}{\mathrm{~nm}}\right)$ electrodes to form a chemiresistive device (as illustrated in Figure $S2c,\mathrm{d})$ . The sensory devices of crystal Ni-HAB were fabricated as above. The as-prepared sensory devices were treated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ on a hot plate for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ before each sensing test.
DFT Calculations. All of the calculations were carried out using the Cambridge Serial Total Energy Package (CASTEP)77 module in the Materials Studio software package.78 The generalized gradient approximation (GGA) method together with the Perdew−Burke− Ernzerhof $\left(\mathrm{PBE}\right)^{79}$ as exchange-correlation functional, and the ultrasoft pseudopotential was employed in both geometry optimizations and electronic property calculations. The Grimme method80,81 was applied to take the van der Waals interaction into consideration. To model the systems of cNi-HAB and aNi-HAB, a slab model in periodic boundary condition (PBC) with the cell size of $26.8\times26.8\times$ $\overset{\cdot}{20}\overset{\cdot}{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ was employed. The energy convergence value between two consecutive steps was chosen as $\mathrm{\overline{{2}}\times10^{-5}\:\mathrm{\overline{{{e}}}V,}}$ and a maximum force was allowed lower than $0.05\ \mathrm{eV}/\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ on each atom. The binding energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{b}}\right)$ was obtained by calculating the energy difference between the total energy of the complex system $\left(\Bar E_{\mathrm{complex}}\right)$ and the sum of individual energy of the Ni-HAB substrate $\left(E_{\mathrm{Ni-HAB}}\right)$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules $\left(E_{\mathrm{H2O}}\right)$ , respectively.
